    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "wait i'm a bit confused. i thought permissions boiled down to the role attached to the lambda. if i add the bucket policy permissions like you said, i still get the error. \n\nis there some other way the access could be getting blocked? like is there some kind of boundary or something else preventing the write?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, there are definitely boundary issues to check. First, ensure CloudWatch Logs resolution is enabled on your S3 buckets and port the `*` prefix to match your full bucket path (`/my-app-logs-bucket/*`) exactly, as the wrong project endpoints will cause a \"not found\" error. Second, verify that your CloudWatch Log group has an attached policy rather than relying solely on the role, and confirm that the specific log stream has its own allow policy if resolution is not used. Finally, double-check that the Lambda execution role has write permission to the CloudWatch Logs service resource itself, not just the S3 bucket.",
